    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1220">

                <pb facs="1167" n="1155" sameAs="frus:frus1943v04/pg1155"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M) Turkey/349: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-02-19T14:00:00+02:00">February 19,
                            1943—2 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 20—4:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>311. Embassy’s 226 February 4.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d1220-f1"/> The Assistant
                    General Manager of the Eti Bank in charge of chrome matters states that Turkish
                    authorities have not yet issued export licenses to the Germans for more than 500
                    tons of chrome. So far as the Embassy is aware no chrome has as yet left Turkey
                    for Germany.</p>
                <p>He confirmed the information contained in the Embassy’s 233 February 5 by
                    apologetically explaining that the shipment in question had been started due to
                    a misunderstanding of instructions at the mine.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
